es {-5.5} to represent a negative category balance\\n\\nThis suggests the underlying logic adds amounts directly with refunds naturally carrying negative values, though there\'s some uncertainty about whether this truly matches legacy requirements versus what\'s being requested right now. The test expectations themselves seem misaligned\\u2014either I\'m misunderstanding something or these tests do need correcting based on the project notes indicating they\'ve become stale over time.\\n\\nI should update both `test_refund_is_subtracted` and `test_refund_only_category_can_be_negative`. Looking back at user specification, refunds genuinely subtract from their category balance even when it\'s a refund-only row\\u2014the test for that case showing `-5.50` reflects the expected negative state after applying only the refund amount to an empty account.\\n\\nThe code currently accepts whatever comes in and adds it directly; with positive amounts stored as-is (whether charge or refund), both get treated identically by `totals[category] += amount`. That\'s why a single row entry like \\"food,charge\\" fails validation\\u2014it rejects negative values upfront.\\n\\nWhen tracing through the logic: \\"food,refund,-20.00\\\\n\\" has kind=\\"refund\\", and if we assume refunds come with negated amounts in input (not CSV format but passed directly), then amount becomes -Decimal(\\"20\\"), and totals[\\"food\\"] ends up as 0 + (-Dec\\n\\n(15)\\n\\nHowever, the test case \\"food,charge,120.00\\\\n\\" followed by \\"food,refund,20.00\\\\n\\" shows both kinds with positive values\\u2014meaning charges add positively while refunds should subtract. The legacy system description says refunds can be input as positive amounts where the logic already handles subtraction for us, which suggests we shouldn\'t negate them ourselves in Python and rely on existing code handling this internally instead of manually negating everything that comes through.\\n\\nLooking at what\'s actually happening: l